.TH POLYTEST 1
.SH NAME
polytest \- test that a Limbo point array reaches the polygon primitives intact
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B polytest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Polytest
checks the four
.IR draw (2)
calls that take an
.B array
of points \(em
.BR Image.poly ,
.BR Image.bezspline ,
.B Image.fillpoly
and
.B Image.fillbezspline
\(em and nothing else does.
.PP
Each case prints
.B ok
or
.BR FAIL ,
and the last line is a count.
.SH "THE DEFECT IT WAS WRITTEN FOR"
.B libinterp/draw.c
handed the array to C by reinterpreting it:
.IP
.EX
/\(** sleazy: we know that Draw_Points have same shape as Points \(**/
fillpolyop(d, (Point\(**)f->p->data, f->p->len, ...)
.EE
.PP
.B "They do not have the same shape on this build."
A Limbo
.B int
is a
.BR WORD ,
which is
.B intptr
here, so
.B "Draw_Point_size"
is
.B 16
\(em two 64\-bit fields \(em against a C
.B Point
of two
.BR s32 ,
eight bytes. Three points stored as the words
.B "2 7 13 3 13 10"
arrive as
.BR "(2,0) (7,0) (13,0)" :
a triangle flattened onto one row.
.PP
.B "The same tree already knew."
The reinterpreting macros in
.B include/draw.h
are
.B commented out
and replaced by field\-by\-field conversions \(em
.B IPOINT
is
.B "((Point){(s32)((p).x),(s32)((p).y)})"
\(em so the scalar path was fixed and the four
.B array
casts were left behind with the comment that justified them still
attached. Every Limbo polygon and spline in the tree drew the wrong
shape.
.SH "HOW IT WAS FOUND, WHICH IS WHY THIS EXISTS"
Not here, and not by anything looking at polygons.
.IR Tkscenetest (1)
rasterises a serialised
.I libtk
render\-node tree from Limbo with
.IR tkrender (2)
and compares it against
.IR libtk 's
own C render of the same tree. Rectangles, rounded corners, borders,
layers and text matched to the byte; the scrollbar's two arrows and the
checkbutton's tick did not. Those are the only two fixtures with a
.B polygon
and a
.B spline
in them.
.SH "EVERY CASE HAS AN ORACLE FROM A DIFFERENT PRIMITIVE"
Because
.B "did it draw something"
cannot tell a correct polygon from a wrong one: the broken version drew
plenty.
.TP
.B "fillpoly of a rect == draw() of it"
A rectangle given as four corners must be the same pixels as the same
rectangle drawn by
.IR draw (2).
Exact, and decisive.
.TP
.B "poly of one segment == line() of it"
.I Line
takes its points as
.B scalars,
through
.BR IPOINT ,
which is the path that was already correct. So the oracle does not
share the defect under test.
.TP
.B "translation invariance"
For the shapes with no second primitive to check them against: the same
points moved by
.RB ( dx , dy )
must draw the same picture moved by
.RB ( dx , dy ).
Reinterpreting the array mixes one point's
.B y
into the next point's
.BR x ,
so moving every point moves some of the shape and not the rest.
.TP
.B "about half its box"
150 to 250 pixels for a triangle in a 20x20 box, because translation
invariance is also satisfied by a shape that is wrong in a
position\-independent way. Loose enough to survive the edge rule, tight
enough that a one\-row collapse and a filled bounding box both fail.
.PP
.B "And SYMMETRY was the wrong property, tried first."
Points mirrored about a vertical axis do
.B not
have to produce a mirrored picture: a scanline fill includes one edge
and excludes the other, so it is asymmetric by about a pixel a row
.B by design.
Three such checks failed against a
.B correct
implementation, and the two exact oracles above are what said the
implementation was fine and the expectation was not.
.SH "THE CONTROL"
Restoring the old cast and rebuilding gives
.BR "2 ok, 7 fail" ,
with the triangle covering
.B "0 pixels"
\(em it vanishes. The two that still pass are the ones that should:
.IR draw ()
does not use the array path, and
.B "the same point array draws the same twice"
is satisfied by being deterministically wrong. A control that makes
every case fail has not shown which cases are sensitive.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/polytest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tkscenetest (1),
.IR tkrender (2),
.IR tknode (2),
.IR draw (2)
.SH BUGS
It does not cover
.B Image.fillpoly 's
winding argument with anything but
.BR ~0 ,
which is what every caller in this tree uses.
.PP
It does not cover a point array long enough to exercise the allocation
in
.IR ipoints (),
nor the failure of that allocation. The fix allocates and converts
rather than reinterpreting, so a polygon now draws nothing at all if
.I malloc
fails, where before it drew garbage; which of those is better is not
in question, but neither is tested.
.PP
It does not cover
.B Image.line
beyond using it as an oracle, and it does not cover
.B Image.arc
or
.BR Image.fillarc ,
which take scalars and are unaffected.
